---

<DocsLayout
  title="Build"
  subtitle="Extend nikcli with plugins, drive it from your own program over HTTP, or embed the whole agent in-process — without forking it."
  description="How to build on nikcli: server and TUI plugins, the HTTP client SDK, and the embedded in-process host."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="paths" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Three ways in</h2>
    <p>
      nikcli is a CLI, a server and a terminal UI that all sit on the same
      <a href="/docs/server-api">typed HTTP API</a>. That means there is no privileged inside: the
      TUI reaches the session engine through the same endpoints your code can. Which path you take
      depends on where your code should run.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-grid not-prose">
      <DocCard title="Extend" eyebrow="Plugins" href="/docs/guides/plugins">
        <p>
          Your code runs <em>inside</em> nikcli. Add tools, intercept the model call, register a
          provider, gate permissions, extend the TUI.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Connect" eyebrow="Client SDK" href="/docs/build/client">
        <p>
          Your program runs outside and drives a nikcli server over HTTP. Scripts, bots, CI jobs,
          alternative interfaces.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Embed" eyebrow="In-process" href="/docs/build/sdk">
        <p>
          nikcli runs inside <em>your</em> process, with no listener and no socket. For products
          that ship the agent rather than talk to one.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
    </div>

    <DocCallout type="info" title="Prefer a walkthrough?">
      <p>
        <a href="/docs/guides">The guides</a> take each of these paths end to end with working
        code — <a href="/docs/guides/plugins">Writing a plugin</a>,
        <a href="/docs/guides/plugin-recipes">Plugin recipes</a>,
        <a href="/docs/guides/api">Using the API</a> and
        <a href="/docs/guides/streaming">Streaming a response</a>.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>

    <DocCallout type="note" title="None of these need a fork">
      <p>
        Every extension point below is a published contract. If you find yourself patching
        <code>packages/nikcli</code> to do something on this page, that is a gap worth reporting
        rather than a fork worth maintaining.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="extend" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Extend — plugins</h2>
    <p>
      A plugin is a module nikcli loads at startup. It receives a client bound to the running
      instance and returns a set of hooks. <code>@nikcli-ai/plugin</code> is the contract package;
      it carries only types and the <code>tool()</code> helper, so it adds nothing to your runtime.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="my-plugin.ts">{`import type { Plugin } from "@nikcli-ai/plugin"
import { tool } from "@nikcli-ai/plugin/tool"

export const MyPlugin: Plugin = async ({ client, directory, $ }) => ({
  tool: {
    deploy: tool({
      description: "Deploy the current branch to staging",
      args: { branch: tool.schema.string().describe("branch to deploy") },
      async execute(args, ctx) {
        ctx.metadata({ title: \`Deploying \${args.branch}\` })
        const out = await $\`./script/deploy.sh \${args.branch}\`.text()
        return { title: "Deployed", output: out }
      },
    }),
  },

  async "tool.execute.before"(input, output) {
    if (input.tool === "bash") output.args.timeout = 120_000
  },
})`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      The hook surface is the interesting part. Beyond <code>tool</code>, a plugin can observe every
      server <code>event</code>, rewrite the parameters and headers of the model call
      (<code>chat.params</code>, <code>chat.headers</code>, <code>chat.request</code>), decide
      permissions (<code>permission.ask</code>), shape the shell environment
      (<code>shell.env</code>, <code>shell.create.before</code>), register an
      <code>auth</code> method or a whole <code>provider</code>, and transform the messages and
      system prompt before they reach the model.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="A shell hook cannot smuggle past approval">
      <p>
        <code>shell.create.before</code> runs before the permission check, and the check is
        performed against the <em>final</em> values the hook wrote. Rewriting a command does not
        inherit the approval granted for the original one.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
    <p>
      TUI feature plugins are a separate runtime with its own contract
      (<code>@nikcli-ai/plugin/tui</code>): they add routes, sidebar panels, dialogs and keybinds to
      the terminal UI, and local ones hot-reload in place while it runs. See
      <a href="/docs/tui-plugins">TUI Feature Plugins</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="connect" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Connect — the client</h2>
    <p>
      <code>@nikcli-ai/sdk</code> is a typed client generated from the same OpenAPI document as the
      <a href="/docs/api">API reference</a>, so every endpoint is a method and contract drift fails
      generation rather than surfacing as a runtime 404.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
npm install @nikcli-ai/sdk
    </DocCode>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`import { createNikcliClient } from "@nikcli-ai/sdk/httpapi"

const client = createNikcliClient({
  baseUrl: "http://127.0.0.1:4096",
  directory: "/Users/me/project",
})

const { data: session } = await client.session.create({ title: "release notes" })
await client.session.prompt({
  sessionID: session!.id,
  parts: [{ type: "text", text: "Summarise what changed since v1.2.0" }],
})`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      Full walkthrough — auth, the result envelope, instance selection, streaming and errors — in
      <a href="/docs/build/client">Client</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="embed" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Embed — in-process</h2>
    <p>
      The embedded host runs the server's request handler in memory. It opens no listener and does
      no network I/O, while keeping the same routing, middleware, handlers, codecs and errors as the
      network client — so code written against the client works unchanged against the embedded host.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`import { NikCli } from "@nikcli-ai/sdk-next"
import { Effect } from "effect"

const program = Effect.gen(function* () {
  const nikcli = yield* NikCli.create({ directory: process.cwd() })
  return yield* Effect.promise(() => nikcli.session.list())
})`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      It also lets you register tools directly, which the HTTP path cannot do — a tool is executable
      code and does not travel over a wire. See <a href="/docs/build/sdk">Embedded SDK</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="choosing" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Which one do I want?</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>If you want to…</th><th>Path</th><th>Package</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Give the agent a new tool</td><td>Plugin</td><td><code>@nikcli-ai/plugin</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Change how the model is called</td><td>Plugin</td><td><code>@nikcli-ai/plugin</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Add a panel or route to the TUI</td><td>TUI plugin</td><td><code>@nikcli-ai/plugin/tui</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Script nikcli from CI or a bot</td><td>Client</td><td><code>@nikcli-ai/sdk</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Build a different interface on top</td><td>Client</td><td><code>@nikcli-ai/sdk</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ship the agent inside your own app</td><td>Embedded</td><td><code>@nikcli-ai/sdk-next</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Generate a client for another language</td><td>OpenAPI</td><td><a href="/docs/api/openapi.json">the document</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Plugins and the client are not exclusive">
      <p>
        A plugin is handed a <code>client</code> bound to the instance it is running in — the same
        <code>NikcliClient</code> the Connect path uses. Reaching for the API from inside a plugin is
        the normal thing to do, not a workaround.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="what" class="doc-section">
    <h2>What people build</h2>
    <div class="doc-grid not-prose">
      <DocCard title="Domain tools" eyebrow="Plugin">
        <p>
          Wrap a deploy script, a migration runner or an internal API so the agent can call it with
          arguments it cannot get wrong.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Policy" eyebrow="Plugin">
        <p>
          Auto-deny a permission class, force a timeout, inject credentials into the shell
          environment, or strip secrets before a request leaves the process.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Custom providers" eyebrow="Plugin">
        <p>
          Register a model provider with its own auth flow and model list — including an internal
          gateway that is not in the public catalogue.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Automation" eyebrow="Client">
        <p>
          Open a session from a webhook, prompt it, wait on the event feed, and post the diff back
          to a pull request.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Alternative interfaces" eyebrow="Client">
        <p>
          The web app, the desktop app and the mobile app are all just clients of these endpoints.
          Yours can be too.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Embedded agents" eyebrow="Embedded">
        <p>
          A product that runs sessions on its own, with your tools registered and no server for a
          user to start.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
    </div>
  </section>

